.SS "server.listeners[].dns.doq"
.PP
DNS over QUIC. DoT and DoH both carry DNS over TCP, so they inherit its head\-of\-line blocking: one slow answer holds up every query behind it on the same connection, which is precisely the shape of a resolver\(aqs traffic. DoQ puts each query on its own QUIC stream, so the answers are independent, while keeping DoT\(aqs privacy properties \[u2014] the same certificate, the same server name, no HTTP layer.
.PP
It shares the listener\(aqs address and certificate, on UDP where DoT and DoH use TCP, and is separated from HTTP/3 by its ALPN (\fBdoq\fR). Each stream carries one query and one answer with a two byte length prefix, as over TCP. The message id is zero on the wire, which RFC 9250 requires: the stream identifies the exchange, so an id would only leak something about the client. \fBqueries_doq\fR counts them.
.PP
\fBquic://host:port\fR is the matching upstream transport, so a chain of resolvers can be QUIC end to end.
.SS "server.listeners[].dns.discovery"
.PP
Discovery of Designated Resolvers (RFC 9462). A client handed this proxy\(aqs address by DHCP has no way to know it also speaks DoT, DoH or DoQ. DDR is the answer: the client asks \fB_dns.resolver.arpa\fR for SVCB records, this resolver answers with its own encrypted endpoints, the client verifies the certificate against the name in the record, and upgrades itself. Nothing is configured on the client.
.PP
.TS
