Every session writes one \fBmqtt\fR line to the access log with the client address, client id, username, version, subscriptions, publications and why it closed. Counters: \fBmqtt_sessions\fR, \fBmqtt_sessions_open\fR, \fBmqtt_published\fR, \fBmqtt_subscribed\fR, \fBmqtt_refused\fR, \fBmqtt_rejected\fR, \fBmqtt_protocol_errors\fR; the matching \fBxproxy_mqtt_*\fR metrics. Refusals are \fBmqtt_denied\fR deny events, so a \fBbans.triggers\fR entry on that reason turns a device walking the topic tree into a ban.
