T{
\fBreject_\:control_\:chars\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
A decoded path or query with a control character (below 0x20,\: or 0x7f),\: NUL included (\fBpath_\:control_\:char\fR,\: \fBquery_\:control_\:char\fR)
T}
T{
\fBreject_\:invalid_\:utf8\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
A decoded path that is not valid UTF\-8: overlong (\fB%c0%af\fR) and truncated sequences (\fBpath_\:invalid_\:utf8\fR)
T}
T{
\fBreject_\:double_\:encoding\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
A path that still holds a percent escape after one decoding (\fB%252e%252e\fR),\: the classic way past a filter that decodes once (\fBpath_\:double_\:encoding\fR)
T}
T{
\fBreject_\:encoded_\:slashes\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
\fB%2F\fR or \fB%5C\fR in the raw path: the routing decoder turns them into separators that the upstream may treat as data (\fBpath_\:encoded_\:slash\fR)
T}
T{
\fBreject_\:backslashes\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
A backslash anywhere in the decoded path (\fBpath_\:backslash\fR). IIS,\: Apache on Windows and .NET read it as a separator while this proxy reads it as an ordinary character,\: so \fB/\:static\e.\:.\:\eadmin\fR is one opaque segment under \fB/\:static\fR here and \fB/\:admin\fR there,\: skipping that route\(aqs access lists,\: filters,\: WAF profile and rate limits
T}
T{
\fBreject_\:path_\:params\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
A \fB;\fR anywhere in the decoded path (\fBpath_\:parameter\fR). Tomcat,\: Jetty,\: JBoss and Spring strip a path parameter from every segment before mapping,\: so \fB/\:admin;x\fR or \fB/\:admin;jsessionid=\:\[u2026]\fR misses this proxy\(aqs \fB/\:admin\fR route and its policy while the origin serves \fB/\:admin\fR. Turn it off for an API that genuinely uses matrix parameters
T}
T{
\fBreject_\:dot_\:segments\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
A \fB.\fR or \fB.\:.\fR segment in the decoded path,\: including the \fB.\:.\:;\fR form servlet containers resolve (\fBpath_\:dot_\:segment\fR). Routing resolves dot segments while the upstream receives the path as sent,\: so \fB/\:static/\:.\:.\:/\:admin\fR would be routed as \fB/\:admin\fR but reach the origin unchanged;\: browsers never send such paths
T}
T{
\fBreject_\:ambiguous_\:framing\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
HTTP/\:1 requests with several differing \fBContent\-\:Length\fR values,\: a \fBContent\-\:Length\fR next to a transfer coding,\: or a coding other than chunked (\fBframing_\:content_\:length\fR,\: \fBframing_\:te_\:cl\fR,\: \fBframing_\:transfer_\:encoding\fR). The Go parser already refuses most of these before the proxy sees them;\: the check closes the rest and makes them visible
T}
T{
\fBunicode\fR
T}	T{
\fBoff\fR,\: \fBnfc\fR,\: \fBnfkc\fR
T}	T{
\fBoff\fR
T}	T{
Fold the decoded path to that form for routing: \fBnfc\fR makes composed and decomposed spellings (\fBcaf\[u00E9]\fR either way) match one route,\: \fBnfkc\fR also compatibility forms such as fullwidth letters (\fB\[uFF55]sers\fR). The upstream receives the original path
T}
.TE
.PP
Turn the strict checks on for applications that never use encoded separators or double encoding legitimately (most APIs), and leave them off in front of applications that carry encoded identifiers in the path; \fBexamples/security/positive\-model.yaml\fR shows the strict set.
.SS "server.error_pages and routes[].error_pages"
.PP
Documents are read at load (at most 1 MiB each) and chosen by exact status (\fB"404"\fR), class (\fB"4xx"\fR, \fB"5xx"\fR) or \fB"default"\fR. A route section replaces the server section entirely for that route. Every status the proxy writes itself goes through the pages: denials (ACL, rate limit, WAF, ban, JWT), unknown host or route, upstream failures (502, 503, 504), static file misses and the like; a gRPC request keeps its gRPC status. Upstream responses pass through unchanged unless their status is listed in \fBintercept_upstream\fR.
.PP
.TS
