Compare the shadow response with the live one and report the differences;\: see below
T}
.TE
.PP
The access log carries \fBmirror: sent\fR, \fBdropped\fR or \fBbody_too_large\fR. Counters: \fBmirror_sent\fR, \fBmirror_dropped\fR, \fBmirror_skipped\fR, \fBmirror_failed\fR; metric \fBxproxy_mirror_total{outcome}\fR. Mirror responses appear in the error log at debug level with their status.
.SS "routes[].mirror.diff"
.PP
Turns the mirror into traffic shadowing for validating a new backend against the current one. The live response is summarised as it streams to the client \[u2014] status, the listed headers, body length and a digest of the first \fBmax_body_bytes\fR \[u2014] without buffering it, and the shadow response is summarised the same way; the two are compared once the client has finished reading. The status is always compared, then the listed headers, then the body; the first category that differs is the reported result. Comparison runs in the background and never affects the client. When the live response does not finish within the mirror \fBtimeout\fR, the comparison is skipped.
.PP
.TS
