T}	T{
Bodies above this size pass through unchanged (1 to 64 MiB)
T}
T{
\fB<phase>.\:rules\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
required
T}	T{
1 to 64 rules of \fB{find,\: replace}\fR (literal) or \fB{regex,\: replace}\fR (RE2;\: \fB$1\fR groups in \fBreplace\fR),\: each with an optional \fBmax\fR count (0 means all)
T}
.TE
.PP
A body is buffered up to \fBmax_bytes\fR and rewritten in memory; a larger body, one the upstream already encoded (\fBContent\-Encoding\fR), a range and any media type outside the list pass through untouched, so the filter never breaks a download. After a change \fBContent\-Length\fR is set and \fBETag\fR and \fBContent\-MD5\fR removed; nothing changes when no rule matched. The access log carries \fBbody_rewrite: request\fR, \fBresponse\fR or \fBrequest,response\fR on lines where a body changed. Response rewriting runs before compression and after the WAF\(aqs response inspection, so the WAF sees the upstream\(aqs bytes and the client sees the rewritten ones. Put the filter on the routes that need it rather than on every route: buffering costs memory per request up to the bound.
.SH "tracing"
.PP
Every request gets a W3C trace context: an incoming \fBtraceparent\fR is continued (its trace id kept, a fresh span id issued, \fBtracestate\fR passed through), otherwise a new trace starts. The proxy records one server span per request (method, path, host, protocol, status, client address, request id, route, upstream, denial reason) and one client span per upstream exchange (endpoint, status, attempts, or the error), sends \fBtraceparent\fR and \fBtracestate\fR to the upstream so its spans hang under the client span, and writes \fBtrace_id\fR, \fBspan_id\fR and \fBtrace_sampled\fR into the access log. Spans are exported as OTLP/HTTP JSON when \fBotlp\fR is set; without it the context is propagated and logged only. Sampling is decided locally by \fBsample_percent\fR; an incoming sampled flag is honoured only with \fBtrust_incoming\fR, so a client cannot push every request into the exporter. \fBxproxyctl telemetry\fR and \fBGET /v1/telemetry\fR show the counters.
.PP
.TS
