Compress the response to a request that carried \fBAuthorization\fR or a \fBCookie\fR. Compressing a body that mixes a secret with attacker\-chosen text leaks the secret through its length,\: one character at a time (BREACH),\: and a request a browser sends with the victim\(aqs cookies is exactly what an attacker can arrange. Turn it on per route (\fBroutes[].\:compress_\:authenticated\fR) where the response holds no secret,\: or where the application already masks its tokens
T}
.TE
.PP
The default \fBtypes\fR are \fBtext/html\fR, \fBtext/plain\fR, \fBtext/css\fR, \fBtext/csv\fR, \fBtext/xml\fR, \fBtext/javascript\fR, \fBapplication/javascript\fR, \fBapplication/json\fR, \fBapplication/ld+json\fR, \fBapplication/manifest+json\fR, \fBapplication/xml\fR, \fBapplication/xhtml+xml\fR, \fBapplication/rss+xml\fR, \fBapplication/atom+xml\fR, \fBimage/svg+xml\fR, \fBapplication/wasm\fR, \fBfont/ttf\fR, \fBfont/otf\fR and \fBapplication/vnd.api+json\fR. Images, video, archives and fonts in \fBwoff2\fR are already compressed and are never listed by default. Compressing responses that mix a secret with attacker\-controlled input in one body exposes the BREACH class of attacks; keep \fBcompress: false\fR on routes that render CSRF tokens next to reflected parameters, or make sure the application masks its tokens.
.SH "cache"
.PP
An in\-memory response cache. The section sizes it; routes opt in with \fBroutes[].cache\fR. The cache is owned by the process, not by a configuration generation, so a reload keeps its contents (and resizes it); a restart empties it.
.PP
.TS
